        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="12.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#intervals" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Intervals by Sealer Type</a>
          <a href="#water-test" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Water Test</a>
          <a href="#other-signs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Other Signs</a>
          <a href="#shortens" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. What Shortens the Interval</a>
          <a href="#build-up" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. The Build-Up Problem</a>
          <a href="#reseal-or-strip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Reseal or Strip First?</a>
          <a href="#schedule" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. A Practical Schedule</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Resealing on a calendar leads to two failure modes: leaving it too long and having to strip, or doing it too often and building a thick plastic-looking film. Testing instead of scheduling avoids both.
        </p>

        <div id="intervals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Intervals by Sealer Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the published ranges as a planning guide, then let the surface override them.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sheltered / Low Traffic</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Exposed / High Traffic</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Driveway</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Penetrating siloxane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-7 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Joint stabilising</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyurethane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-8 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-6 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two patterns are worth noticing. Penetrating sealers last longer than film sealers in every column, because there is no film to wear through; they deplete gradually rather than failing visibly. And every product roughly halves its life going from a sheltered patio to a driveway, because ultraviolet exposure and tyre traffic are both punishing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solvent-based acrylics generally outlast water-based ones by a year or so, partly because they penetrate a little further before forming their film. That is a real advantage, though it comes with stronger odour during application and a greater need for a solvent-resistant sprayer, which our paver sealing cost guide accounts for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Treat all of these as starting assumptions rather than answers. A south-facing patio in Arizona and a shaded one in Oregon will diverge enormously from the same product, and the difference between them is larger than the difference between any two products in the table.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first reseal is often sooner than the subsequent ones on a porous patio, because the first application partly disappears into the pavers and the second builds on a partly sealed surface. Do not be surprised if a patio needs attention at two years and then holds for four.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Water Test, Which Beats Any Calendar
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the whole of the practical answer and it takes half a minute.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pour a cup of water onto the pavers in a few different places: a traffic path, a sheltered corner, and somewhere in the open. Watch what it does over about a minute. If it beads up and sits on the surface, the sealer is working. If it spreads and soaks in, darkening the paver, that area has lost its protection and is due.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test several spots because sealer does not wear evenly. The path from the back door to the barbecue will fail years before the corner under the bench, and the parts in full afternoon sun will fail before the shaded side. A patio is usually part due and part fine, which is exactly the information the calendar cannot give you.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What the Water Does</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Condition</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Action</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Beads tightly, sits for minutes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealer in good order</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Recheck next season</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Beads weakly, slowly spreads</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Starting to wear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plan to reseal within a year</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soaks in within a minute, darkens</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Protection gone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reseal now</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soaks in and surface looks dull or patchy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Film degraded</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check whether stripping is needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Beads but surface is cloudy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moisture or build-up issue</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Diagnose before recoating</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Do the test twice a year, in spring and autumn, and note which areas fail first. After a couple of cycles you will know your own patio's pattern, which is more useful than any general interval and lets you plan the job for good weather rather than reacting to a problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caveat: a penetrating sealer can still be providing stain resistance after water beading has faded, because the two properties deplete at different rates. On a penetrating sealer the water test is a useful early warning rather than a hard deadline, and the practical test is whether spills are still wiping up rather than staining.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Other Signs It Is Due
          </h2>

          <p>
            Beyond the water test, four visible indicators worth watching for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Colour Has Gone Flat</h3>

          <p>
            If you sealed for the wet look, the most obvious sign is that the wet look has faded. Colour enhancement depends on an intact film, so the richness fading is a direct report on the film's condition, and it usually appears in the sunniest areas first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Traffic Paths Are Visible</h3>

          <p>
            A visible difference in sheen between where people walk and where they do not means the film has worn through in the traffic areas. This is the point to act, because continuing to use a partly worn film leads to the patchy appearance that eventually needs stripping rather than recoating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joint Sand Is Washing Out</h3>

          <p>
            If you used a joint stabilising sealer and the sand is starting to move again in heavy rain or under a leaf blower, the binding has depleted. This often shows before the surface water test fails, because the joints see more water flow than the paver faces.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stains Are Starting to Take</h3>

          <p>
            The functional test. If a spilled drink now leaves a mark where it used to wipe off, the protection has gone regardless of what the surface looks like. On a driveway, the equivalent is an oil drip darkening the paver rather than sitting on top.
          </p>

          <p>
            What is not a sign that it is due: moss or algae growing on the surface. That indicates a shaded damp surface and needs cleaning, not resealing, and sealing over biofilm traps it. Clean first, reassess afterwards.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="shortens" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Shortens the Interval
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your patio is failing faster than the published range, one of these is usually why.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ultraviolet exposure is the largest single factor. Acrylic films degrade in sunlight, and a fully exposed south-facing patio can lose a year or more against a shaded one with the identical product. Nothing can be done about this except choosing a more UV-stable product and accepting a shorter cycle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Traffic, particularly vehicle traffic. Tyres, and especially turning tyres, scuff and lift a film far more aggressively than feet. This is why driveway columns in every interval table are the shortest, and why a turning circle in a driveway will often be the first area to need attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            Freeze-thaw cycling. Water that gets into a partly worn film and then freezes expands and lifts it, so climates with many freeze-thaw transitions are harder on sealers than uniformly cold ones. A patio in a region that hovers around freezing all winter suffers more than one that simply stays frozen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Harsh cleaning. Pressure washing at high pressure strips sealer directly, and aggressive cleaners, particularly acidic ones, degrade acrylic films. If you pressure wash a sealed patio at 3,000 PSI every spring, you are removing your sealer annually and then wondering why it does not last.
          </p>

          <p>
            De-icing salts. Chlorides are hard on both sealer and concrete, and a driveway or path that gets salted every winter will need resealing considerably sooner. If salt is unavoidable, a penetrating sealer generally tolerates it better than a film.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What Costs You Years</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Full sun</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">UV degrades acrylic film</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Turning tyres</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Hardest wear on a driveway</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3,000 PSI</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Strips sealer while cleaning</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Road salt</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Chlorides attack film and concrete</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="build-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Build-Up Problem Nobody Warns You About
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the failure mode of doing it too often, and it is as real as leaving it too long.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every coat of a film sealer adds thickness. Recoat on a three-year cycle without ever removing anything and after four cycles you have a film several times its original thickness. At that point the patio stops looking like enhanced stone and starts looking like stone under plastic: the sheen sits visibly on top, the texture is gone, and the colour looks artificial.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thick films also fail differently and worse. They are more prone to trapping moisture, more likely to blush white, and when they do peel they come off in sheets rather than wearing thin. A heavily built-up patio is also much harder to strip than a single aged coat, because the stripper has more to work through.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoiding it is straightforward once you know. Apply thinner coats when resealing than you did on bare pavers, because you are topping up rather than building from nothing. A single thin coat is usually correct for a reseal where the original required two. And accept that every third or fourth cycle is likely to be a strip-and-reseal rather than a recoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers do not have this problem at all, which is one of their under-appreciated advantages. They deplete rather than accumulate, so resealing simply replenishes and there is nothing to build up or strip. Over a twenty-year patio life that difference is substantial in both money and unpleasant weekends.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already have build-up, the tell is a surface that looks plasticky, feels slick, and where the joints have begun to fill with sealer at the edges. That patio needs stripping before its next coat rather than another layer on top.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reseal-or-strip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Reseal or Strip First?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The decision at each cycle, and getting it wrong in either direction is costly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recoat directly when the existing film is sound, thin, the same chemistry as what you are applying, and simply worn. This is the normal case on a well-maintained patio and it is a straightforward clean, dry and one thin coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Strip first when the film is peeling or flaking, when it has gone cloudy or hazy, when there is visible build-up from several cycles, when you are changing chemistry, or when you do not know what is on there. Applying a solvent-based sealer over a water-based one, or any sealer over a degraded film, produces a failure rather than a repair.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Existing Condition</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Action</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Worn thin, otherwise sound, same product</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean and recoat, one thin coat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Worn unevenly, traffic paths visible</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean and recoat, may need spot work first</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cloudy, hazy or blushed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Diagnose first; usually strip</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling or flaking anywhere</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip fully</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thick, plasticky, joints filling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip fully</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unknown product or age</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip, or test a small area first</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are unsure whether the existing film is compatible, test in a hidden square foot. Apply the new sealer, let it cure for several days, and check for lifting, wrinkling, clouding or poor adhesion. That test costs nothing and prevents the version of this mistake that covers an entire patio.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stripping is a genuinely unpleasant job involving chemical stripper, pressure washing and a great deal of rinsing, and it roughly doubles the cost of that cycle. That is the strongest practical reason to test with water twice a year and act at the right moment rather than discovering the problem two years late.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Practical Schedule
          </h2>

          <p>
            What this looks like as an actual routine rather than a set of principles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Twice a year, in spring and autumn, do the water test in four or five spots and note the result. It takes five minutes and it is the entire monitoring requirement. Write the date and the result somewhere you will find it, because the trend across years is what tells you your patio's real interval.
          </p>

          <p>
            Annually, clean the patio properly with an appropriate cleaner and moderate pressure. Keep the pressure washer at a wide tip and well back, because aggressive washing removes sealer and shortens the cycle you are trying to extend. Treat moss and algae promptly rather than letting them establish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Top up joint sand as needed, which on most patios is every couple of years in the areas that see water flow. Doing this before it becomes obvious keeps the base stable and prevents the deeper problems that come from washed-out joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            When the water test fails in the traffic areas, plan the reseal for the next stretch of settled warm weather rather than doing it immediately in whatever conditions exist. A reseal done properly in good conditions lasts its full term; one rushed in marginal weather may not last two years. Our guide on how to seal pavers covers the conditions that matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            And every third or fourth cycle, assess honestly whether the film has built up enough to need stripping rather than another coat. Catching that at the right point, rather than after the patio has started looking plasticky, keeps the job manageable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep a note of the product you used and the date, somewhere durable such as inside a garage cupboard door. The commonest cause of a difficult reseal is nobody remembering what is on the patio, which forces either a test patch or a precautionary strip. Thirty seconds with a marker saves that.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing on a Calendar Instead of Testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Published intervals span two to seven years because exposure, traffic and climate matter more than the product does. The water test takes thirty seconds: if water beads the sealer is working, and if it soaks in and darkens the paver, that area is due. Test several spots, because wear is never even.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating Every Cycle Without Ever Stripping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each film coat adds thickness, and after four cycles the patio looks like stone under plastic, with the sheen sitting visibly on top. Thick films also blush and peel more readily and are far harder to strip. Apply thinner coats when resealing, and expect a strip every third or fourth cycle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure Washing a Sealed Patio at High Pressure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Washing at 3,000 PSI strips sealer directly, so an annual aggressive clean means you are removing your own sealer every year and then blaming the product for not lasting. Use a wide tip at moderate pressure, held well back, and use appropriate cleaners rather than force.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying a Different Sealer Chemistry Over an Existing Film</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Solvent over water-based, or any sealer over a degraded or unknown film, produces lifting, wrinkling or poor adhesion rather than a repair. If you are changing product or do not know what is on the patio, either strip or test a hidden square foot and let it cure for several days first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Moss Growth as a Sign It Needs Resealing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moss and algae on the surface indicate a shaded damp patio, not a depleted sealer, and sealing over biofilm traps it under the film. Clean the growth off first, then run the water test to see whether the sealer underneath is actually due.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should pavers be sealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Every two to five years for film sealers and three to seven for penetrating ones, depending heavily on sun exposure, traffic and climate. Driveways sit at the short end because turning tyres are hard on a film. Rather than following a calendar, run the water test twice a year and reseal when it fails in the traffic areas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know when to reseal my pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pour a cup of water on the surface in several spots. If it beads and sits, the sealer is working. If it spreads, soaks in and darkens the paver within a minute, that area has lost protection. Other signs are faded colour, visible sheen differences along traffic paths, joint sand moving again, and spills starting to stain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal pavers too often?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it causes a real problem. Each film coat adds thickness, so recoating every cycle without ever stripping builds up a layer that eventually looks like plastic over stone, traps moisture more readily and peels in sheets. Apply thinner coats on reseals and plan a strip every third or fourth cycle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does paver sealer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water-based acrylics typically two to four years, solvent-based three to five, and penetrating siloxanes four to seven, with every figure roughly halving on a driveway. Full sun, vehicle traffic, freeze-thaw cycling, de-icing salt and aggressive pressure washing all shorten it considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to strip the old sealer before resealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not if the existing film is thin, sound, simply worn, and the same chemistry as what you are applying. You must strip if it is peeling, cloudy, visibly built up, or if you are changing product type or do not know what is on there. If unsure, test the new sealer on a hidden square foot and let it cure for several days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a penetrating sealer need resealing as often?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Less often, typically every four to seven years, and it has no build-up problem because it depletes rather than accumulating. Note that water beading can fade while stain resistance remains, so on a penetrating sealer the water test is an early warning rather than a deadline; the practical test is whether spills still wipe up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I reseal the whole patio or just worn areas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Whole patio, in nearly every case. Spot-resealing leaves a visible difference in sheen between new and old areas that is more obvious than the wear you were fixing. The exception is a genuinely separate zone, such as a driveway apron or a set of steps, which can reasonably be treated on its own cycle.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

