        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Plan on two to five years for a film sealer and three to seven for a penetrating one, then ignore the calendar and let the water test decide. Thirty seconds, four or five spots, twice a year: if water beads the sealer is working, and if it soaks in and darkens the paver, that area is due. Wear is never even, so testing the traffic path and the sheltered corner separately tells you far more than any published interval.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The two mistakes are symmetrical. Leave it too long and the film degrades past recoating into a strip-and-start-again job. Recoat too eagerly and you build a thick plastic-looking layer that blushes, peels in sheets and is harder to strip than a single aged coat. Thinner coats on reseals, a strip every third or fourth cycle, and a note of what product you used will keep a sealed patio looking right for its whole life.
          </p>
